    {
      "id": "mrmcse5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Sources of Information about Earth's Interior"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Earth's interior is structured into three main concentric layers: the Crust, Mantle, and Core.",
        "Information about the interior is derived from direct sources (rocks, volcanic eruptions) and indirect sources (seismic waves, gravity, magnetic fields).",
        "Seismic waves—specifically P-waves and S-waves—help delineate the state and density of internal layers.",
        "The crust is categorized into thin, dense Oceanic Crust (SIMA) and thicker, lighter Continental Crust (SIAL).",
        "Major seismic discontinuities (e.g., Mohorovicic and Gutenberg) mark distinct boundaries between varying rock densities."
      ],
      "id": "ctzxm1l"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "plskdif",
      "content": "Direct Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7eeafe7",
      "content": "• Deep mining operations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jq3i6im",
      "content": "• Scientific drilling projects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ssu5v6d",
      "content": "• Volcanic eruptions which bring material from inside the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "71nq2it",
      "content": "Indirect Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ecc0v07",
      "content": "• Seismic waves generated by earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nnqyfb5",
      "content": "• Study of meteorites."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1emcy43",
      "content": "• Gravitational and magnetic field studies."
    },
    {
      "id": "nr5xmj5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Seismic Waves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qu6hin5",
      "content": "• Seismic waves are energy waves generated during earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g9y6dm3",
      "content": "• They help scientists understand the structure of Earth's interior."
    },
    {
      "id": "c39n5us",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Wave Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Nature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Characteristics</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>P Waves (Primary)</p>",
            "<p>Compressional (body waves)</p>",
            "<p>Travel through solids, liquids and gases; fastest</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>S Waves (Secondary)</p>",
            "<p>Transverse (body waves)</p>",
            "<p>Travel only through solids</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>R Waves (Surface)</p>",
            "<p>Surface waves</p>",
            "<p>Large amplitude and greater destruction</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>L Waves (Surface)</p>",
            "<p>Surface waves</p>",
            "<p>Cause maximum destruction during earthquakes</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "xlx1j0e",
